<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionPrefix>0.3.5</VersionPrefix>
    <AssemblyName>ChromaDB.VectorData</AssemblyName>
    <PackageId>ChromaDotNet.VectorData</PackageId>
    <RootNamespace>$(AssemblyName)</RootNamespace>
    <TargetFrameworks>net10.0;net8.0;netstandard2.0;net462</TargetFrameworks>
    <IsAotCompatible Condition="$([MSBuild]::IsTargetFrameworkCompatible('$(TargetFramework)', 'net8.0'))">true</IsAotCompatible>

    <Title>Chroma provider for Microsoft.Extensions.VectorData</Title>
    <Description>Microsoft.Extensions.VectorData provider for Chroma and Chroma Cloud, built on ChromaDotNet.Client. Community project, not affiliated with Chroma.</Description>
    <PackageTags>chroma chromadb chroma-cloud vector-database vectordata ai</PackageTags>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.3.5: upserting a record that exists replaces it: Chroma merges the metadata of an upsert, so the provider reads which keys the record has stored and deletes those that are now null or an empty list, the BM25 vectors of the texts that are now null, and the document of a null text; before, the old values stayed, and the filters, like the namespaces of the TextSearchStore of Semantic Kernel, still found them. A new record gets no null keys, which Chroma Cloud counts in its 32 keys per record. A text of the property stored as the document longer than the 8,182 bytes of a metadata value of Chroma Cloud is stored in the document only, and the BM25 index of that property takes its text from the document. A DateTimeOffset is stored in UTC, and filters compare dates as == does in C#: instants for DateTimeOffset, ticks for DateTime. It needs ChromaDotNet.Client 2.9.1, which writes a double with an integer value as a double, so that comparisons and Contains find it, and sends a long chain of conditions as one list. 0.3.4: the package is tagged vector-database. 0.3.3: the documentation of CreateBm25Indexes says that ChromaDotNet.Client computes the BM25 vectors, and Chroma applies the inverse document frequency. 0.3.2: the vector store registers with dependency injection also with NativeAOT and trimming, and the warnings of the collection registrations name the way that works there, the dynamic collection; the README of the package describes the registrations, the score of hybrid search and the supported types. 0.3.1: a collection offers hybrid search through GetService only with CreateBm25Indexes, where it can work: the TextSearchStore of Semantic Kernel, which chooses hybrid search through GetService, searches by vector again elsewhere, as with 0.2.0. 0.3.0: hybrid search on Chroma Cloud: HybridSearchAsync fuses the ranks of the vector search and of a BM25 search of the keywords; CreateBm25Indexes creates a BM25 index for each full-text indexed string property, and collections created by other Chroma clients with a chroma_bm25 index work too; it needs ChromaDotNet.Client 2.8.0, which reads in pages and writes in batches on Chroma Cloud by itself. 0.2.0: registration with the options of the client, like those of Chroma Cloud; reads in pages of the batch size of the client; the full-text property is also stored as the Chroma document, and Contains on it filters its text. 0.1.2: full-text indexed properties are accepted, as Semantic Kernel and Agent Framework declare them; filters on the key; an existing collection with another space throws instead of giving wrong scores; empty lists are not stored; Contains over an empty list matches no record; a collection created again elsewhere is looked up again. 0.1.0: the first release. All the releases: https://github.com/ChromaDotNet/ChromaDB.VectorData/releases</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>
  
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Linq.AsyncEnumerable" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.AI.Abstractions" />
    <PackageReference Include="ChromaDotNet.Client" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0' Or '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net462' ">
    <PackageReference Include="System.Text.Json" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net462' ">
    <Reference Include="System.Net.Http" />
    <Using Include="System.Net.Http" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="ChromaDB.VectorData.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>